<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1911 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bread and Roses</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A 1911 poem, later a song, saying that working people need fair pay and also the good things of life, such as music and books.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>James Oppenheim</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Caroline Kohlsaat (1917); later settings by Mimi Fariña (1974), John Denver (1988) and Renate Fresow</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>American women's suffrage and labor movements</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem published December 1911; first set to music 1917</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by James Oppenheim (published December 1911); the first music, by Caroline Kohlsaat, dates from 1917, while later settings such as Mimi Fariña's (1974) are in copyright)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Bread and Roses&quot; began as a slogan of the American women's suffrage movement. Its first known appearance is in an article by the suffrage activist Helen Todd in The American Magazine of September 1911. In June 1910 Todd, a state factory inspector, had joined an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> campaign across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> for women's right to vote, speaking for working women on wages, conditions and hours. A young hired girl in whose family's house she stayed told her she had liked best the idea that women's votes would bring everyone bread and flowers too. Todd made of it the phrase &quot;Bread for all, and Roses too&quot;: bread meaning home, shelter and security, and roses meaning music, education, nature and books. The slogan was carried on a banner in the Chicago garment workers' strike of 1910 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>'s successful suffrage campaign of 1911.</p><p>The poet James Oppenheim took up the phrase in his poem &quot;Bread and Roses&quot;, published in The American Magazine in December 1911 with the note that the slogan was one of &quot;the women in the West&quot;. It was reprinted in The Survey in July 1912 and in The Public in October 1912. When Upton Sinclair printed it in his anthology The Cry for Justice in 1915, its first appearance in a book, the slogan was credited to young girls carrying a banner in a parade of strikers at Lawrence, Massachusetts. That textile strike, from January to March 1912, united dozens of immigrant communities under the Industrial Workers of the World and was led largely by women; it is now often called the &quot;Bread and Roses strike&quot;, although a number of historians think the story of the banner is not historical. In 1912 the trade unionist Rose Schneiderman spread the phrase further in speeches for women's suffrage, saying that the woman worker must have bread, but roses too.</p><p>Caroline Kohlsaat first set the poem to music in 1917, for the River Forest Women's Club, where she directed the chorus. Her song moved to the picket lines: by the 1930s women sang it while they fed and supported strikers outside the factories. It also became a college song. Graduating seniors at Mount Holyoke College have sung it in the Laurel Parade since 1932, and it is central to the Step-Sings of Bryn Mawr College, whose Summer School for Women Workers, begun in 1921, brought a hundred factory, mill and sweatshop workers each year to study.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the song reached a wider audience through the magazine Sing Out! in January 1952. Mimi Fariña set the poem again in 1974, and her version was recorded by Judy Collins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, Ani DiFranco and others; John Denver set it to another melody in 1988. It was translated into German and Russian. The 2014 film Pride shows a Welsh mining community singing it during the miners' strike of 1984–1985, and the slogan has named the Bread and Roses Heritage Festival in Lawrence, the emblem of the Democratic Socialists of America and the organisation Pan y Rosas.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The poem gives voice to working women marching for more than wages: they ask for fair pay and also for dignity and the beauty of life. Its mood is hopeful and resolute.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As we come marching, marching, in the beauty of the day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A million darkened kitchens, a thousand mill-lofts gray</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Are touched with all the radiance that a sudden sun discloses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For the people hear us singing, &quot;Bread and Roses, Bread and Roses.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As we come marching, marching, we battle, too, for men—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For they are women's children and we mother them again.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our days shall not be sweated from birth until life closes—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hearts starve as well as bodies: Give us Bread, but give us Roses.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As we come marching, marching, unnumbered women dead</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Go crying through our singing their ancient song of Bread;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Small art and love and beauty their trudging spirits knew—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Yes, it is Bread we fight for—but we fight for Roses, too.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As we come marching, marching, we bring the Greater Days—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The rising of the women means the rising of the race.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>No more the drudge and idler—ten that toil where one reposes—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But a sharing of life's glories: Bread and Roses, Bread and Roses.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: James Oppenheim, The American Magazine (December 1911); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bread_and_Roses" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="f33k8hYO4JE" data-title="Bread and Roses: Judy Collins (Mimi Fariña's setting)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Judy Collins (Mimi Fariña's setting)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Judy Collins - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f33k8hYO4JE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (Caroline Kohlsaat)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (Mimi Fariña)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (Christian Wolff)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano piece based on the strike song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (John Denver)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1988</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new musical setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (Renate Fresow)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German setting, later sung in Peter Maiwald's translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">They all sang 'Bread and Roses' (Si Kahn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song quoting the title in its refrain</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (Kirill Medvedev, sung by Arkadiy Kots)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Russian translation set to Kohlsaat's music</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pride (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a scene</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bread and Roses (documentary, Sahra Mani)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2023</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title taken from the slogan</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Slogan &quot;Bread for all, and Roses too&quot; (Helen Todd)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1911</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the poem's title and idea</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Saying attributed to Galen of Pergamon</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a close parallel quoted by reformers, possibly an earlier source of the idea</span></li></ul></section>
